# Ordinary menu UIA resource comparison, 2026-09-27

The corrected library exposes all 12/128 ordinary menu rows to UIA in both B4 and B5; A1/A2 exposed zero. B4/B5 each passed `test.ps1 -Suites MenuAccessibility`, and their suite receipt SHA matches the executable inventory. A1/A2 are the expected semantic red baseline, but each completed all 24 resource observations before its assertion failed. All four runs used the same one-line text workload, 476 × 322 px popup at 96 DPI, 12 and 128 rows, four open/close cycles per size. See `resource-derived-observations.json` for every before/open/closed delta and `analyze-resources.ps1` for the calculation.

| Warm open minus immediately preceding closed state (cycles 1–3; six observations per cohort) | A1/A2 mean | B4/B5 mean | Candidate increase |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 12 rows, process private bytes | 683,349 B | 703,147 B | 19,798 B |
| 12 rows, enumerated heap busy bytes | 249,392 B | 272,608 B | **23,216 B** |
| 128 rows, process private bytes | 311,979 B | 763,904 B | 451,925 B |
| 128 rows, enumerated heap busy bytes | 396,655 B | 627,760 B | **231,105 B (0.220 MiB)** |

The added **live heap** cost scales at about 1.8–1.9 KiB per row over these two sizes, consistent with retained controls and snapshot records. It is a meaningful new cost, not a surface counter or a per-frame allocation. The process-private deltas are much noisier (the 128-row candidate warm-open observations range from 213 KiB to 1.41 MiB), so 451,925 B is an observed mean, not a precise per-menu allocation. The first 12-row open in each fresh process increases private bytes by about 64 MiB for general UI/graphics initialization; it is excluded from the warm comparison. GDI and USER counts did not differ between cohorts; normal warm opens added six handles, and cycle-3 128-row opens added eleven in both cohorts.

The extra heap is released on close in this bounded fixture. Warm 128-row closed-minus-before heap busy means are 41,327 B (A) and 41,414 B (B), a difference of 87 B; 12-row means are -31,930 B (A) and -31,679 B (B), a difference of 251 B. Negative values reflect unrelated heap reuse between adjacent samples. Cycle-3 128-row closed-minus-before handles are +5 in both cohorts, so that retention is not newly introduced by the candidate. Process private bytes after close fluctuate in both directions and do not establish a persistent candidate leak across four cycles.

The common offscreen complex-UI benchmark is not a menu workload. Across A1/A2 versus B4/B5 (ten rounds each), median clean FPS is 2626 versus 2534 and median dirty FPS is 561 versus 579; clean C++ allocations remain zero, dirty median is 2160, and surface bytes remain 3,686,400. B4 contains a low clean-FPS round (1859), while B5 is within the baseline spread, so these unpaired summaries do not establish a stable frame-performance effect from the menu change. The separate **actual** aborted B1/B2 performance comparisons are retained under `excluded-aborted-performance/`: both flagged clean `composeCpuP95Ms` against the then-selected baseline round (0.0112→0.0123 ms, +9.82%; and 0.0112→0.0120 ms, +7.14%). They must not be silently waived or confused with successful UIA suite runs. Their copied B1/B2 suite logs/receipts predate those invocations and report the baseline executable SHA; B3 lacked a complete collector receipt. `correction-metadata.json` records these exclusions and SHA-256 values of the copied raw reports.

The decision is whether the measured row-scaling cost is acceptable for ordinary menus, especially larger menus. Options are to optimize per-row control/snapshot storage and remeasure; narrow optional materialization without losing required visible/disabled/noncommand accessibility semantics; or defer the library change and its dependent consumer UIA journey. The 12/128 fixture alone cannot predict a 1000-row cost or authorize a broader performance waiver.
